<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What the FileProvider will hand out.

    Deliberately broad, because a file manager is asked to open whatever the
    user is looking at and there is no narrower path that still covers "the file
    they tapped". `cache-path` is here for the copies pulled down from a server,
    which live in the cache until something opens them and are not on external
    storage at all.

    `root-path` is the one that matters, and it was missing.

    `external-path` resolves to getExternalStorageDirectory(), which is the
    *primary* volume and nothing else. An SD card is mounted somewhere like
    /storage/1A2B-3C4D, a USB stick somewhere else again — neither is under that
    root, so getUriForFile threw IllegalArgumentException for every file on one.
    The app offers SD cards as shortcuts on its own home screen, so the failure
    was reachable in two taps: open a file on the card, and be told nothing on
    this phone could open it. The same applied to sharing.

    Broad here is not the same as exposed. The provider is not exported, so
    nothing can ask it for a URI; the app mints one for a single file at the
    moment the user asks for that file to be opened, and hands it over with a
    read grant that lasts as long as the receiving activity. That is the same
    posture every file manager on the platform takes, and it is the only one
    that can answer "open this" for an arbitrary path.

    The narrower entries are kept below it. They cost nothing, and they document
    the two places that are always in play.
-->
<paths>
    <root-path name="root" path="/" />
    <external-path name="external" path="." />
    <cache-path name="downloads" path="remote/" />
</paths>
